The Consumer Protection Act, 2019 recognises that consumers have a class‑based right to be protected, promoted and enforced; to be free from violations of their rights under the Act; to be free from unfair trade practices; and to be free from false or misleading advertisements. It also provides for inquiries, investigations, complaints, intervention before the District/State/National Commissions, and a review of factors that inhibit the enjoyment of consumer rights, including safeguards available under any other law. [Source 5]

**Statutory text (Section 18, Consumer Protection Act, 2019)**  
> “18. (1) The Central Authority shall—  
> (a) protect, promote and enforce the rights of consumers as a class, and prevent violation of consumers rights under this Act;  
> (b) prevent unfair trade practices and ensure that no person engages himself in unfair trade practices;  
> (c) ensure that no false or misleading advertisement is made of any goods or services which contravenes the provisions of this Act or the rules or regulations made thereunder;  
> (d) ensure that no person takes part in the publication of any advertisement which is false or misleading.  
> (2) Without prejudice to the generality of the provisions contained in sub‑section (1), the Central Authority may, for any of the purposes aforesaid,—  
> (a) inquire or cause an inquiry or investigation to be made into violations of consumer rights or unfair trade

