Yes. A party may apply directly to the arbitral tribunal for interim measures of protection during the arbitral proceedings (or after the award but before enforcement) under Section 17(1) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 [Source 1].  

**Statutory text (Section 17):**  
“(1) A party may, during the arbitral proceedings or at any time after the making of the arbitral award but before it is enforced in accordance with section 36, apply to the arbitral tribunal—  
(i) …;  
(ii) for an interim measure of protection in respect of any of the following matters, namely:—  
(a) the preservation, interim custody or sale of any goods which are the subject‑matter of the arbitration agreement;  
(b) securing the amount in dispute in the arbitration;  
(c) the detention, preservation or inspection of any property or thing which is the subject‑matter of the dispute in arbitration …;  
(d) interim injunction or the appointment of a receiver;  
(e) such other interim measure of protection as may appear to the arbitral tribunal to be just and convenient…” [Source 3].  

**Comparison with court‑based remedy:**  
A party may also seek interim measures from a civil court under Section 9(1) (apply to a court before, during, or after proceedings but before enforcement) [Source 2]. The tribunal’s power under Section 17 mirrors the court’s power under Section 9, but the application is made to the arbitral tribunal instead of the court.  

**IPC/BNS:** Not applicable; the sources pertain only to the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, so no IPC/BNS comparison is required.  

**Practical implication:** Parties can obtain urgent relief (e.g., preservation of assets, injunctions) without first approaching a court, which may expedite the arbitration process and reduce procedural delays. However, if the tribunal declines or is unable to grant the requested measure, the party retains the option to approach the court under Section 9.

